# Dialyzer baseline.
#
# The discipline, which is narrower than it used to be: an entry here is a warning
# somebody looked at and decided to keep, and the reason is written above it. It is not a
# place to put a warning because it is new, because a line moved, or because it is
# inconvenient. Adding one is a change that needs the same defence as any other.
#
# What this replaced: 188 entries, of which 114 no longer matched anything at all. Line
# numbers had drifted out from under them, so those warnings were firing unfiltered while
# their entries sat here looking like coverage. `mix dialyzer` was red against its own
# baseline. Twenty more were `Unknown function`/`Unknown type` against modules that exist
# and are called — PLT coverage, now fixed in `mix.exs` rather than filtered.
#
# Matching is `String.contains?/2` against a warning as `mix dialyzer --format dialyzer`
# prints it, so an entry pins a line number and will stop matching when code moves. That
# is deliberate: a stale entry should stop working rather than quietly widen.
#
# KNOWN GAP, and it is the one that let the last baseline rot. Nothing detects a dead
# entry in this file. `Unnecessary Skips: 0` in the dialyzer output is not a check that
# passed — dialyxir only tracks which filters fired for an `.exs` pattern file, and this
# is a plain-text one, so `list_unused_filters: true` is inert here. Verified by adding a
# filter naming a file that does not exist: still `Unnecessary Skips: 0`, still green.
# So an entry whose line number drifts stops filtering and stops being counted, in
# silence, and the warning it named starts firing unnoticed. Closing this properly means
# converting the file to the `.exs` pattern form, where `list_unused_filters: true`
# actually fails the build on a dead entry. Worth doing once the held section below is
# empty. Until then: when you touch this file, re-run `mix dialyzer --format dialyzer`
# with it emptied and diff, rather than trusting the skip count.
#
# Also: reproduce failures with the default formatter or `--format dialyzer`.
# `--format short` throws on this tree — dialyxir 1.4.7 has no case for OTP's
# `:exact_compare` warning and dies formatting it, before any filtering happens.

